// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [Agency Next Algorithm.note]         search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Events are treated from the outside without any knowledge of the organization of the data inside.
   The caller to AgencyNext requests an event that needs to occur, and does nothing when given null.

   Inside the database: all events may be searched for an instance that is of the type requested, or
      a basic/complex sorting mechanism can be employed to reduce "time to occur" logic as desired.
   The important point here is the caller doesn't need to know the database organization/methods,
   this allows someone disgusted by the crude memory management here to write a better version.  Go!
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   input:    a pointer to ssE_pAboutEvVoid (Null) or
                    a pointer to the ssE_pAboutEv that was returned the last call
             a type that the caller is interested in right now

    the actions of the two input variables are independent:  must restart with Null if change type
   ----------
   pointer:  given null, start search with "first" instance of EvInfos data structures.
                (the database does not need to be sorted by Time-to-Occur for there to be a "first").
             given !null, start search at instance after the EvInfo pEvency pointed to by caller.
                (this should be the pointer that was returned by the last call to AgencyNext).
   type:     of Ireq, data, or Time
   ----------
    result:  given null or !null, finding no Active Events of matching Type, return null.
             on match: return ssE_pAboutEv from the Active EvInfo array element for Agency to consider.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ssE_pEvInfo_t ssA_EvAg_AgencyNext (ssE_pEvInfo_t pEvInfo, ssE_EvType_t  eventType)
//
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The caller doesn't know about data structures and algorithms used, only the pointer returned
    by a call to this function.  That pointer is provided to signal "next after this one, please".
    This algorithm doesn't really use the current pEvInfo; a linked-list-based algorithm would
    certainly have pointers to "next after this one" because that is what a linked list is.
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    To signal that the resolution should start clean at the top is a NULL pointer or, the local
    variable (retained over a series of calls, or, cross-Agency) incremented out of range, which
    it does at the end of every series of calls to return a Next, when there is no Next Event.
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    if ((pEvInfo == ssE_pEvInfoNull) || (working_lcl_i > SSE_EVINFO_MAX_I))
//
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    An FSM must use memory retained across Time, sending messages to a future Self really.
      Current value of lcl_i is important to generate a pointer to an event to the caller.
      ++lcl_i before return is important to start next call's search at the next element.
    written explicitly here with pre-increment and extra pointer variable: be obvious.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    pEvInfo = &ssE_EvInfos[working_lcl_i];
//
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    This is the rule that the Agency pointer returned points to an Agency Api active Event.
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    An Event's Agency Api FSM must be in the "On and Running" state to be returned by Next.
    An "On and Running" Event's Agency Run FSM must be in the "Agencying" state to continue.
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Software Engineering: for the sake of completion without concern for optimization, check
    as many values as possible before performing an indirect call to a function out in space.
    A developer who was extremely careful could make sure that every EvInfo considered has
    all of the checked values (active:on_running:event:agencying) when AgencyNext searches,
    which would eliminate the time and space spent every time an Event is ready for Agency.
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (pEvInfo->Alloc.active)
//
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The Agency Api FSM does not know Agency Run FSM states, even in "On and Running" state.
    The Agency Run FSM does not know Agency Api FSM states, asssumes "On and Running" state.
    This is where the two FSMs must be in a coordinated set of states to be granted Agency.
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (pEvInfo->ssE_pAboutEv->Runfsm_EvType == eventType)
//

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
